Decision: validated inline images use Beaver's existing fixed 1,100-token image estimate for both the prepared input and its capacity value; Base64 transport characters are never counted as model text. Remote image URLs remain unbounded. The same Rust constant is emitted into the generated TypeScript contract and the prepared breakdown assigns image tokens to Messages.
Reason: Base64 is a wire encoding, not the provider's vision-token representation. Treating 836,940 encoded characters as text inflated the reproduced request from 57,928 to 262,785 capacity tokens, falsely crossed the 90% threshold for a 258,400-token window, launched automatic compression, and made the retained-image candidate fail. Existing image count, decoded-byte, format, and provider limits remain the separate transport safety boundary.
Product decision: the context panel keeps one unlabeled total as previously approved; no estimation badge is added. Provider-specific image tokenization remains deferred until Beaver has a verified per-route authority.
Validation: the exact four-image regression failed first with tokens=57,928 and capacity_tokens=262,785, then passed with equal counts below 60,000 and no automatic compression at 258,400. Five prepared-context tests, four breakdown tests, twelve orchestrator tests, 41 focused frontend tests, TypeScript, frontend lint, Rust clippy -D warnings, all 3,350 frontend tests and scripts, all 5,606 Rust library tests, 51 Beaver CLI tests, macOS parent-death tests, contract generation, and graphify update . passed. Beaver Dev compiled and launched from the CEF development bundle; the frontend loaded the generated runtime constant.
